The browser half of this package repackages dsh-recall, used under the MIT
License. The original project lives at:

    https://github.com/omdsh-dev/dsh-recall

    vendor/client.js is its published bundle verbatim; scripts/build.mjs
    adapts it through asserted exact-string rewrites, so a changed upstream
    line fails the build rather than silently shipping something unintended.
    The rewind button sits inside the user message, which means shadowing the
    framework's keyed user-bubble renderer and reproducing the bubble —
    content blocks, image gallery, lightbox and all. Upstream does that
    faithfully and redoing it would be risk without benefit.

The host half is NOT upstream's. Upstream calls `session.recall()`, which is
core runtime support rather than a plugin seam — its own README says so — and
no published @deepseek-ai/dsh-session has ever shipped it: rc.7 has no such
method and a grep of rc.8 finds no match either. Its host is therefore dead
code on any real harness, and vendor/index.js is not carried.

src/ implements the same feature with public API only. The model-visible
history is a *surface* projected over the append-only log, and a surface event
may enter as a replacement — `{ op: 'replace', start, end }` substitutes one
node for a whole range. Compaction uses exactly this to swap a stretch of
history for a summary, and the type documentation states that "any
surface-replacing producer may use it". Rewinding replaces
[boundary .. last surface node] with a single marker, so `deriveMessages()`
stops projecting the rewound turns.

Two consequences worth stating plainly:

  - The replacement op cannot empty a range; it always leaves exactly one node.
    That node is a marker message saying the conversation was rewound and how
    many messages went with it. The model reads it, which is deliberate: a
    silently shorter history invites the model to re-derive conclusions it has
    no record of reaching.

  - Nothing is deleted. Every original event stays in the log, and no file on
    disk is touched, so code written during a rewound turn stays exactly as it
    is. This is the one real difference from the rewind in Claude Code, which
    also restores the working tree.

Renamed to @my-dsh/rewind, and the route with it: POST /recall is now
POST /rewind. The client bundle's __ModuleLoader__ id follows the package name,
because the web boot manifest looks a bundle up by that name. The `recall` node
kind and the `session/recall` event definition in the browser half are left
alone: they are the runtime's names, not this package's.

English only. Upstream registers a Simplified Chinese dictionary alongside
English; the English one is now registered under both locale ids.

The `/rewind` command and its message picker are not upstream at all. They live
in the companion @my-dsh/rewind-picker package, which drives this route.

